  <h2>Power</h2>

  <p>
    The Arduino Uno R3 can be powered through the USB connection or by an
    external power supply. The board automatically selects the appropriate
    power source.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>USB Power:</strong> 5V</li>
    <li><strong>Recommended External Input:</strong> 7V - 12V</li>
    <li><strong>External Input Limit:</strong> 6V - 20V</li>
    <li><strong>Power Jack:</strong> 2.1mm center-positive plug</li>
    <li><strong>3.3V Output:</strong> Maximum 50 mA</li>
    <li><strong>GND:</strong> Ground connection</li>
    <li><strong>VIN:</strong> External power input</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For stable operation, an external supply of <strong>7V to 12V</strong>
    is recommended. Supplying more than 12V may cause the onboard voltage
    regulator to overheat.
  </p>


  <h2>Memory</h2>

  <p>
    The ATmega328 microcontroller provides sufficient memory for a wide range
    of Arduino projects.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Flash Memory:</strong> 32 KB</li>
    <li><strong>SRAM:</strong> 2 KB</li>
    <li><strong>EEPROM:</strong> 1 KB</li>
    <li><strong>Bootloader:</strong> 0.5 KB of Flash is used by the bootloader</li>
  </ul>


  <h2>Input and Output</h2>

  <p>
    The Arduino Uno R3 features 14 digital I/O pins and 6 analog input pins,
    providing flexible connectivity for sensors, LEDs, switches, displays,
    motors, and other electronic components.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Digital I/O:</strong> 14 pins</li>
    <li><strong>PWM:</strong> Pins 3, 5, 6, 9, 10 and 11</li>
    <li><strong>Analog Inputs:</strong> A0 - A5</li>
    <li><strong>Digital Pin Current:</strong> Maximum 40 mA per I/O pin</li>
    <li><strong>Analog Resolution:</strong> 10-bit</li>
    <li><strong>SPI:</strong> Pins 10, 11, 12 and 13</li>
    <li><strong>Serial:</strong> Pins 0 (RX) and 1 (TX)</li>
    <li><strong>TWI/I2C:</strong> A4 (SDA) and A5 (SCL)</li>
  </ul>


  <h2>Communication</h2>

  <p>
    The Uno R3 supports several communication interfaces for connecting the
    board to computers, sensors, modules, displays, and other microcontrollers.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>USB:</strong> Programming and serial communication</li>
    <li><strong>UART:</strong> TTL serial communication</li>
    <li><strong>I2C / TWI:</strong> Supported through the Wire library</li>
    <li><strong>SPI:</strong> Supported through the SPI library</li>
  </ul>


  <h2>Programming</h2>

  <p>
    The Arduino Uno R3 can be programmed using the Arduino software through
    the USB connection. The ATmega328 comes with a bootloader that allows
    new programs to be uploaded without requiring an external hardware
    programmer.
  </p>

  <p>
    The board also provides an <strong>ICSP header</strong> for programming
    the microcontroller directly when required.
  </p>


  <h2>Features</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>ATmega328 microcontroller</li>
    <li>14 digital input/output pins</li>
    <li>6 PWM outputs</li>
    <li>6 analog input pins</li>
    <li>16 MHz clock speed</li>
    <li>USB connectivity</li>
    <li>Power jack</li>
    <li>ICSP header</li>
    <li>Reset button</li>
    <li>Built-in TX, RX and Pin 13 LEDs</li>
    <li>USB overcurrent protection</li>
  </ul>


  <h2>Applications</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Robotics projects</li>
    <li>STEM and educational projects</li>
    <li>Electronics prototyping</li>
    <li>Home automation projects</li>
    <li>Sensor-based projects</li>
    <li>Motor control projects</li>
    <li>LED and display projects</li>
    <li>Embedded system development</li>
    <li>IoT and automation prototypes</li>
  </ul>


  <h2>Package Includes</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>1 × Arduino Uno R3 High-Quality Edition</li>
    <li>1 × USB Programming Cable</li>
  </ul>

